    "reasoning": "All 16 final answers agree with their own captured worked solutions. For q-64723 and q-64724, the speeds are respectively sqrt(2) and sqrt(16t^6+9t^4+9). For q-127602, the speed is t^2+1, whose integral from -1 to 2 is 6. For q-77220, differentiation gives (4t cos(2t),4t sin(2t)), so the speed is 4t on [1,2] and the length is 6. Its source contains an intermediate typographical error that drops the doubled trigonometric arguments and leaves sine without an argument; the corrected calculation still confirms its final answer. For q-76906 and q-33779, integrating the speeds 2sqrt(2)t and 2t gives 4sqrt(2) and 8. The inverse-trigonometric integrals follow by substituting x/3, sqrt(3)x, x/3, 3x, sqrt(3)x, 4x, and 2x respectively; the arcsec derivatives include the required absolute value. For q-15405, y'=4sin(2x), giving the displayed arc-length integrand. For q-6001, y'=ln(x)+1, and expanding 1+(ln(x)+1)^2 gives the selected expression. For q-85665, the speed is (e^x+e^(-x))/2, whose integral over [0,ln(2)] is 3/4. There is no captured grade or earlier answer to reconcile; confirmation rests on the supplied worked solutions and independent mathematics.",
